  "slug": "condensation-dynamics-e624",
  "summary": "Molecular and mesoscale simulations now model how phase separation, hydration, and kinase cycling organize condensates and chromatin.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a computational approach: coarse-grained or mesoscale simulations used to model phase-separated and phase-organized assemblies rather than to describe them only as endpoints. One item applies Langevin dynamics to coarse-grained phase-separating scaffold proteins, showing induced attraction between membrane receptors relevant to synaptic bistability. A second uses molecular simulations of TDP-43 low-complexity domain condensates, where kinase-phosphatase cycling by CK1δ and calcineurin drives self-sustained condensate oscillations. A third couples chromatin elasticity, water transport, and epigenetic reaction-diffusion in a non-equilibrium mesoscale model that predicts hydration-driven chromatin phase organization. The common thread is explicit non-equilibrium driving, through reaction cycles or coupled transport, of condensate-like organization. The items address different systems and mechanisms and do not share a single model.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-31"
}
